 "One drachma is left": "",
 "Two are left": "",
 "Socrates in Athens. A philosopher who has to get out of the house.": "",
 "Chapter 11: find Socrates’ house, west of the Agora of Athens.": "",
 "Morning in the House: Socrates wants you to distract Xanthippe": "",
 "(whispering) Friend! Keep my wife busy for a moment. The Agora is full of people who think they know things.": "",
 "Then the goat can wait too. The roof leaks, the loom is broken, my son can’t count, and my husband would rather ask strangers what courage is.": "",
 "Climb the ladder and re-lay the roof tiles": "",
 "Mend the loom with a pulley": "",
 ", npc: xanthippe, text: ": "",
 "Teach Lamprocles a sum": "",
 "The Escape: Socrates has slipped out! Keep up with him through the streets": "",
 "Shoemaker! What is justice?": "",
 "Tell me, friend, what is courage?": "",
 ", npc: n, text: ": "",
 ", npc: socrates, text: ": "",
 "You caught up with Socrates.": "",
 "Caught: Xanthippe has found you in the Agora": "",
 "You ask everyone in Athens what justice is. Ask me. Is it just that I mend the roof while you talk about the soul?": "",
 "…That is a very good question.": "",
 "I know. I’ve had twenty years to think of it.": "",
 "Side with Socrates: the Agora needs his questions.": "",
 "Of course. Here. Take your key.": "",
 "Side with Xanthippe: home first, today.": "",
 "Then I shall question my sons. They have never been questioned. It will be terrible for them.": "",
 "Thank you. Truly. Here.": "",
 "A deal: Crito pays a roofer, mornings at home, afternoons in the Agora.": "",
 ", npc: crito, text: ": "",
 "This key was given to me by an old foreigner with sad eyes. He said: “One day a young man with my face will need it. Make him earn it.”": "",
 "Ignition Key": "",
 "A part of the machine disguised as a door key, kept by Xanthippe on a cord. Part 11 of 13.": "",
 "Perhaps you are not looking for him, friend. Perhaps you are looking for yourself.": "",
 "An old foreigner with sad eyes": "",
 "Xanthippe’s key came from an old foreigner with sad eyes: “One day a young man with my face will need it. Make him earn it.” Socrates: “Perhaps you are looking for yourself.”": "",
 "Socrates of Athens": "",
 "He wrote nothing; he asked questions, examining definitions until confident people discovered they did not know what they thought they knew. “The unexamined life is not worth living.” Almost everything we know of his wife Xanthippe comes from later, often hostile writers.": "",
 "Ignition Key recovered: ": "",
 "Clue: a young man with his face.": "",
 "Next: Plato’s cave, outside Athens.": "",
 "Grandpa Leo’s test track": "",
